<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Backup and transfer rules for API 31+. The API 30-and-below equivalent is
   backup_rules.xml, and the two must stay in step.

   The session store is excluded from BOTH flows, and the second one is the interesting
   case:

   - cloud-backup: the encrypted refresh token is restored onto a device whose Keystore
     never held the key that encrypts it. The file is undecryptable, so backing it up
     achieves nothing and moves a credential off the device for no benefit.

   - device-transfer: the same argument, and it is worth stating separately because the
     instinct is that a direct phone-to-phone transfer should carry everything. It cannot
     carry this. Keystore keys are hardware-bound and do not move between devices, by
     design – that is precisely the property that makes storing the token there worth
     doing. A transferred file would fail to decrypt on first launch on the new phone.

   In both cases the user signs in again on the new device, which is the correct and
   expected behaviour for a credential.

   See KeystoreTokenCipher for the in-app handling of an undecryptable blob, which covers
   this even if these rules are ever lost to a careless edit.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <exclude
            domain="file"
            path="datastore/session.preferences_pb" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <exclude
            domain="file"
            path="datastore/session.preferences_pb" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
